artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider Monte Carlo event generators used to simulate charged- and neutral-current neutrino-nucleus scattering at intermediate energies (roughly 0.1-10 GeV), where the inclusive cross section receives contributions from quasielastic single-nucleon knockout, two-particle two-hole (multinucleon) excitations driven by meson-exchange currents and short-range correlations, resonant and non-resonant single- and multi-pion production, deep inelastic scattering, and is further modified by nuclear ground-state dynamics (e.g. spectral functions, random phase approximation, relativistic mean field) and by hadronic final-state interactions in the residual nucleus.

Devise a consistent and computationally efficient framework for incorporating modern microscopic theoretical descriptions of all of the above channels into a unified Monte Carlo event generator that:

(a) treats each exclusive hadronic final state with kinematics and interference effects compatible with the underlying inclusive cross-section model (i.e., no double counting between quasielastic, two-particle two-hole, and pion-production channels, and a coherent treatment of axial-vector form factors of the nucleon and of baryon resonances);

(b) couples the primary interaction vertex to a quantum-mechanically consistent intranuclear cascade or transport model for final-state interactions, with a clearly specified prescription for off-shell propagation, Pauli blocking, and medium modifications;

(c) provides well-defined, theoretically motivated tunable parameters (rather than ad hoc rescalings of axial masses or normalization factors) that experimental collaborations can constrain from data without sacrificing predictivity;

(d) is efficient enough to generate the large event samples (>= 10^8 events) required for precision neutrino-oscillation analyses.

Formulate such a framework explicitly, prove (or argue rigorously) that it reduces to the established microscopic inclusive cross sections in the appropriate limits, and demonstrate that fits to existing neutrino-nucleus scattering data do not require unphysical parameter values.

